in august 2020 the world's hottest  temperature  ever might have been recorded 54 degrees  celsius in death valley california  scorching deserts aren't the only places  feeling the heat  on the other extreme a siberian town  reached 38 degrees this year  a new high above the arctic circle  places around the world are undergoing  more frequent and  intense hot weather in 2018  two weeks of heat in japan caused more  than a thousand deaths  and more than 70 000 hospitalizations  as temperatures rise many are at risk  from elderly people to those who work  outside  on farms and construction sites  a federal report predicts tens of  thousands of deaths by the end of the  century  in the united states alone but  researchers say a majority of  related deaths and injuries can be  prevented  with straightforward solutions  it starts with understanding how heat  stroke sets in  this occurs when the core body reaches  about 40 degrees celsius  or three degrees higher than most  people's average temperature  when internal sensors tell the brain  that the body is warming  blood vessels close to the skin expand  to promote cooling  if this doesn't lower the body's  temperature the heart will start pumping  more blood to redirect  fluids and the body will begin sweating  humans can sweat up to two liters an  hour  under strenuous conditions if a person  becomes dehydrated  the adrenal gland releases a fluid  regulating hormone in the kidneys  to reduce urine production  too much fluid loss makes it harder for  the heart to maintain  blood pressure if blood pressure  decreases  the body's internal temperature can  climb rapidly  people with weaker hearts are more  vulnerable to heat stroke  a crucial step is immersion in cold  water within 30 minutes of heat stroke  or exhaustion  restoring hydration is also important  but in one study participants thirst was  quenched  well before they replenish their lost  fluids  making sure to drink the right amount of  water to avoid dehydrat